-- title:
--   Non-Eisenstein corner of H¹(Γ_H(N),𝒪) is parabolic
-- statement:
--   Fix $N \ge 1$ (nonzero) and a subgroup $H \le (\mathbb Z/N)^\times$, a set $S$ of natural numbers, a commutative ring $\mathcal O$ and a field $k$ that is an $\mathcal O$-algebra. Let $\mathrm{Gen}\,N\,S$ be the generator type with constructors $T_\ell$ ($\ell$ prime, $\ell \notin S$, $\ell \nmid N$), $U_q$ ($q$ prime, $q \mid N$) and $\langle d\rangle$ ($d \in (\mathbb Z/N)^\times$), acting on $H^1 = \mathrm{Hom}(\mathrm{Additive}\,\Gamma_H(N), \mathcal O)$, where $\Gamma_H(N) \le \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ is the image of the preimage of $H$ under $\Gamma_0(N) \to (\mathbb Z/N)^\times$; the operators are the corestriction operators `heckeTL` for $T_\ell$ and $U_q$ and the diamond operators for $\langle d\rangle$. Assume these endomorphisms commute pairwise (`hcomm`), fix a function $\bar\theta : \mathrm{Gen}\,N\,S \to k$, and let $\mathbb T' \le \mathrm{End}_{\mathcal O}(H^1)$ be the $\mathcal O$-subalgebra generated by their range. Let $Sp$ be an idempotent splitting of $\mathbb T'$: complete orthogonal idempotents $e_i$ and maximal ideals $\mathfrak m_i$, $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,n$, exhausting the maximal ideals, with $e_i \in \mathfrak m_j \iff i \ne j$; fix an index $i_0$. Let $\ell$ be a prime with $\ell \notin S$, $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \equiv 1 \pmod N$, and assume $T_\ell - (\ell + 1) \notin \mathfrak m_{i_0}$ as an element of $\mathbb T'$. Assume further (`hstab`) that each generator maps the submodule of parabolic homomorphisms — those $\varphi$ vanishing on every $\gamma \in \Gamma_H(N)$ with $\mathrm{tr}(\gamma)^2 = 4$ — into itself. Then every $\varphi$ in the $\mathbb T'$-submodule $e_{i_0} \cdot H^1$ is parabolic.
--
--   This is the statement that the corner $e_{i_0}H^1(\Gamma_H(N),\mathcal O)$ cut out by a non-Eisenstein maximal ideal of the Hecke algebra consists of parabolic cohomology classes, the $\Gamma_H$-level version of the standard comparison between full and parabolic cohomology away from Eisenstein primes. It is used in the construction of the Hecke-module ideal and duality input for the Taylor–Wiles argument, via [`CohCarrier.exists_ideal_H1_top_to_dual_baseChange_heckeTorsion_jZero_of_isAbsolutelyIrreducible`](thm.html#CohCarrier.exists_ideal_H1_top_to_dual_baseChange_heckeTorsion_jZero_of_isAbsolutelyIrreducible).
